WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.775
Try it.

2
00:00:00.775 --> 00:00:03.855
Lab 1 runs on the kit's
fixtures, with no tenant:

3
00:00:03.855 --> 00:00:09.350
a fictional Contoso export in the shape
release three point one point oh writes.

4
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.824
Step one reads the export's metadata,

5
00:00:11.824 --> 00:00:16.113
from the jay-son file the tool
writes under its zt-export folder.

6
00:00:16.113 --> 00:00:19.636
It's version three point one
point oh, tenant Contoso,

7
00:00:19.636 --> 00:00:23.650
thirty-three tests, and it ends
with the end-of-jay-son marker.

8
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:28.338
That marker is the last thing the tool
writes, so a file without it is incomplete.

9
00:00:28.338 --> 00:00:32.871
Step two counts the tool's
statuses: one error, sixteen failed,

10
00:00:32.871 --> 00:00:37.224
one investigate, eight passed,
one planned, and six skipped.

11
00:00:37.224 --> 00:00:38.812
Now think about a pass rate.

12
00:00:38.812 --> 00:00:43.124
It would divide eight by a total that
moves with licensing and connections.

13
00:00:43.124 --> 00:00:46.364
Connect one more service next
month and the rate changes,

14
00:00:46.364 --> 00:00:48.225
though nothing in the tenant did.

15
00:00:48.225 --> 00:00:50.710
That's why the program doesn't report one.
